ion: `lines 117-119`\n  - first observed in trace A: `line 119`\n  - observed lines: `119`\n  - trace activity: 3 events, 1 calls, 1 line events, 1 returns, 0 exceptions, max depth 3\n  - sample executed lines:\n    - `line 119`: `return (x,)`"}, {"content": "<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>\n[\"sliding_window\", \"sliding_window_view\", \"as_strided\"]\n</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>\nnumpy/lib/stride_tricks.py\n</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n##Searching for term \"sliding_window\", \"sliding_window_view\", \"sliding_window sliding_window_view\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `numpy/polynomial/_polybase.py:ABCPolyBase.window`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n109 | def window(self):\n110 |         pass\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"sliding_window\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `numpy/linalg/linalg.py:matrix_power`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n554 | def matrix_power(a, n):\n555 |     \"\"\"\n556 |     Raise a square matrix to the (integer) power `n`.\n557 | \n558 |     For positive integers `n`, the power is computed by repeated matrix\n559 |     squarings and matrix multiplications. If ``n == 0``, the identity matrix\n560 |     of the same shape as M is returned. If ``n < 0``, the inverse\n561 |     is computed and then raised to the ``abs(n)``.\n562 | \n563 |     .. note:: Stacks of object matrices are not currently supported.\n564 | \n565 |     Parameters\n566 |     ----------\n567 |     a : (..., M, M) array_like\n568 |         Matrix to be \"powered\".\n569 |     n : int\n570 |         The exponent can be any integer or long integer, positive,\n571 |         negative, or zero.\n572 | \n573 |     Returns\n574 |     -------\n575 |     a**n : (..., M, M) ndarray or matrix object\n576 |         The return value is the same shape and type as `M`;\n577 |         if the exponent is positive or zero then the type of the\n578 |         elements is the same as those of `M`. If the exponent is\n579 |         negative the elements are floating-point.\n580 | \n581 |     Raises\n582 |     ------\n583 |     LinAlgError\n584 |         For matrices that are not square or that (for negative powers) cannot\n585 |         be inverted numerically.\n586 | \n587 |     Examples\n588 |     --------\n589 |     >>> from numpy.linalg import matrix_power\n590 |     >>> i = np.array([[0, 1], [-1, 0]]) # matrix equiv. of the imaginary unit\n591 |     >>> matrix_power(i, 3) # should = -i\n592 |     array([[ 0, -1],\n593 |            [ 1,  0]])\n594 |     >>> matrix_power(i, 0)\n595 |     array([[1, 0],\n596 |            [0, 1]])\n597 |     >>> matrix_power(i, -3) # should = 1/(-i) = i, but w/ f.p. elements\n598 |     array([[ 0.,  1.],\n599 |            [-1.,  0.]])\n600 | \n601 |     Somewhat more sophisticated example\n602 | \n603 |     >>> q = np.zeros((4, 4))\n604 |     >>> q[0:2, 0:2] = -i\n605 |     >>> q[2:4, 2:4] = i\n606 |     >>> q # one of the three quaternion units not equal to 1\n607 |     array([[ 0., -1.,  0.,  0.],\n608 |            [ 1.,  0.,  0.,  0.],\n609 |            [ 0.,  0.,  0.,  1.],\n610 |            [ 0.,  0., -1.,  0.]])\n611 |     >>> matrix_power(q, 2) # = -np.eye(4)\n612 |     array([[-1.,  0.,  0.,  0.],\n613 |            [ 0., -1.,  0.,  0.],\n614 |            [ 0.,  0., -1.,  0.],\n615 |            [ 0.,  0.,  0., -1.]])\n616 | \n617 |     \"\"\"\n618 |     a = asanyarray(a)\n619 |     _assert_stacked_2d(a)\n620 |     _assert_stacked_square(a)\n621 | \n622 |     try:\n623 |         n = operator.index(n)\n624 |     except TypeError as e:\n625 |         raise TypeError(\"exponent must be an integer\") from e\n626 | \n627 |     # Fall back on dot for object arrays. Object arrays are not supported by\n628 |     # the current implementation of matmul using einsum\n629 |     if a.dtype != object:\n630 |         fmatmul = matmul\n631 |     elif a.ndim == 2:\n632 |         fmatmul = dot\n633 |     else:\n634 |         raise NotImplementedError(\n635 |             \"matrix_power not supported for stacks of object arrays\")\n636 | \n637 |     if n == 0:\n638 |         a = empty_like(a)\n639 |         a[...] = eye(a.shape[-2], dtype=a.dtype)\n640 |         return a\n641 | \n642 |     elif n < 0:\n643 |         a = inv(a)\n644 |         n = abs(n)\n645 | \n646 |     # short-cuts.\n647 |     if n == 1:\n648 |         return a\n649 | \n650 |     elif n == 2:\n651 |         return fmatmul(a, a)\n652 | \n653 |     elif n == 3:\n654 |         return fmatmul(fmatmul(a, a), a)\n655 | \n656 |     # Use binary decomposition to reduce the number of matrix multiplications.\n657 |     # Here, we iterate over the bits of n, from LSB to MSB, raise `a` to\n658 |     # increasing powers of 2, and multiply into the result as needed.\n659 |     z = result = None\n660 |     while n > 0:\n661 |         z = a if z is None else fmatmul(z, z)\n662 |         n, bit = divmod(n, 2)\n663 |         if bit:\n664 |             result = z if result is None else fmatmul(result, z)\n665 | \n666 |     return result\n```\n\nFound function `numpy/linalg/linalg.py:svd`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1483 | def svd(a, full_matrices=True, compute_uv=True, hermitian=False):\n1484 |     \"\"\"\n1485 |     Singular Value Decomposition.\n1486 | \n1487 |     When `a` is a 2D array, it is factorized as ``u @ np.diag(s) @ vh\n1488 |     = (u * s) @ vh``, where `u` and `vh` are 2D unitary arrays and `s` is a 1D\n1489 |     array of `a`'s singular values. When `a` is higher-dimensional, SVD is\n1490 |     applied in stacked mode as explained below.\n1491 | \n1492 |     Parameters\n1493 |     ----------\n1494 |     a : (..., M, N) array_like\n1495 |         A real or complex array with ``a.ndim >= 2``.\n1496 |     full_matrices : bool, optional\n1497 |         If True (default), `u` and `vh` have the shapes ``(..., M, M)`` and\n1498 |         ``(..., N, N)``, respectively.  Otherwise, the shapes are\n1499 |         ``(..., M, K)`` and ``(..., K, N)``, respectively, where\n1500 |         ``K = min(M, N)``.\n1501 |     compute_uv : bool, optional\n1502 |         Whether or not to compute `u` and `vh` in addition to `s`.  True\n1503 |         by default.\n1504 |     hermitian : bool, optional\n1505 |         If True, `a` is assumed to be Hermitian (symmetric if real-valued),\n1506 |         enabling a more efficient method for finding singular values.\n1507 |         Defaults to False.\n1508 | \n1509 |         .. versionadded:: 1.17.0\n1510 | \n1511 |     Returns\n1512 |     -------\n1513 |     u : { (..., M, M), (..., M, K) } array\n1514 |         Unitary array(s). The first ``a.ndim - 2`` dimensions have the same\n1515 |         size as those of the input `a`. The size of the last two dimensions\n1516 |         depends on the value of `full_matrices`. Only returned when\n1517 |         `compute_uv` is True.\n1518 |     s : (..., K) array\n1519 |         Vector(s) with the singular values, within each vector sorted in\n1520 |         descending order. The first ``a.ndim - 2`` dimensions have the same\n1521 |         size as those of the input `a`.\n1522 |     vh : { (..., N, N), (..., K, N) } array\n1523 |         Unitary array(s). The first ``a.ndim - 2`` dimensions have the same\n1524 |         size as those of the input `a`. The size of the last two dimensions\n1525 |         depends on the value of `full_matrices`. Only returned when\n1526 |         `compute_uv` is True.\n1527 | \n1528 |     Raises\n1529 |     ------\n1530 |     LinAlgError\n1531 |         If SVD computation does not converge.\n1532 | \n1533 |     See Also\n1534 |     --------\n1535 |     scipy.linalg.svd : Similar function in SciPy.\n1536 |     scipy.linalg.svdvals : Compute singular values of a matrix.\n1537 | \n1538 |     Notes\n1539 |     -----\n1540 | \n1541 |     .. versionchanged:: 1.8.0\n1542 |        Broadcasting rules apply, see the `numpy.linalg` documentation for\n1543 |        details.\n1544 | \n1545 |     The decomposition is performed using LAPACK routine ``_gesdd``.\n1546 | \n1547 |     SVD is usually described for the factorization of a 2D matrix :math:`A`.\n1548 |     The higher-dimensional case will be discussed below. In the 2D case, SVD is\n1549 |     written as :math:`A = U S V^H`, where :math:`A = a`, :math:`U= u`,\n1550 |     :math:`S= \\\\mathtt{np.diag}(s)` and :math:`V^H = vh`. The 1D array `s`\n1551 |     contains the singular values of `a` and `u` and `vh` are unitary. The rows\n1552 |     of `vh` are the eigenvectors of :math:`A^H A` and the columns of `u` are\n1553 |     the eigenvectors of :math:`A A^H`. In both cases the corresponding\n1554 |     (possibly non-zero) eigenvalues are given by ``s**2``.\n1555 | \n1556 |     If `a` has more than two dimensions, then broadcasting rules apply, as\n1557 |     explained in :ref:`routines.linalg-broadcasting`. This means that SVD is\n1558 |     working in \"stacked\" mode: it iterates over all indices of the first\n1559 |     ``a.ndim - 2`` dimensions and for each combination SVD is applied to the\n1560 |     last two indices. The matrix `a` can be reconstructed from the\n1561 |     decomposition with either ``(u * s[..., None, :]) @ vh`` or\n1562 |     ``u @ (s[..., None] * vh)``. (The ``@`` operator can be replaced by the\n1563 |     function ``np.matmul`` for python versions below 3.5.)\n1564 | \n1565 |     If `a` is a ``matrix`` object (as opposed to an ``ndarray``), then so are\n1566 |     all the return values.\n1567 | \n1568 |     Examples\n1569 |     --------\n1570 |     >>> a = np.random.randn(9, 6) + 1j*np.random.randn(9, 6)\n1571 |     >>> b = np.random.randn(2, 7, 8, 3) + 1j*np.random.randn(2, 7, 8, 3)\n1572 | \n1573 |     Reconstruction based on full SVD, 2D case:\n1574 | \n1575 |     >>> u, s, vh = np.linalg.svd(a, full_matrices=True)\n1576 |     >>> u.shape, s.shape, vh.shape\n1577 |     ((9, 9), (6,), (6, 6))\n1578 |     >>> np.allclose(a, np.dot(u[:, :6] * s, vh))\n1579 |     True\n1580 |     >>> smat = np.zeros((9, 6), dtype=complex)\n1581 |     >>> smat[:6, :6] = np.diag(s)\n1582 |     >>> np.allclose(a, np.dot(u, np.dot(smat, vh)))\n1583 |     True\n1584 | \n1585 |     Reconstruction based on reduced SVD, 2D case:\n1586 | \n1587 |     >>> u, s, vh = np.linalg.svd(a, full_matrices=False)\n1588 |     >>> u.shape, s.shape, vh.shape\n1589 |     ((9, 6), (6,), (6, 6))\n1590 |     >>> np.allclose(a, np.dot(u * s, vh))\n1591 |     True\n1592 |     >>> smat = np.diag(s)\n1593 |     >>> np.allclose(a, np.dot(u, np.dot(smat, vh)))\n1594 |     True\n1595 | \n1596 |     Reconstruction based on full SVD, 4D case:\n1597 | \n1598 |     >>> u, s, vh = np.linalg.svd(b, full_matrices=True)\n1599 |     >>> u.shape, s.shape, vh.shape\n1600 |     ((2, 7, 8, 8), (2, 7, 3), (2, 7, 3, 3))\n1601 |     >>> np.allclose(b, np.matmul(u[..., :3] * s[..., None, :], vh))\n1602 |     True\n1603 |     >>> np.allclose(b, np.matmul(u[..., :3], s[..., None] * vh))\n1604 |     True\n1605 | \n1606 |     Reconstruction based on reduced SVD, 4D case:\n1607 | \n1608 |     >>> u, s, vh = np.linalg.svd(b, full_matrices=False)\n1609 |     >>> u.shape, s.shape, vh.shape\n1610 |     ((2, 7, 8, 3), (2, 7, 3), (2, 7, 3, 3))\n1611 |     >>> np.allclose(b, np.matmul(u * s[..., None, :], vh))\n1612 |     True\n1613 |     >>> np.allclose(b, np.matmul(u, s[..., None] * vh))\n1614 |     True\n1615 | \n1616 |     \"\"\"\n1617 |     import numpy as _nx\n1618 |     a, wrap = _makearray(a)\n1619 | \n1620 |     if hermitian:\n1621 |         # note: lapack svd returns eigenvalues with s ** 2 sorted descending,\n1622 |         # but eig returns s sorted ascending, so we re-order the eigenvalues\n1623 |         # and related arrays to have the correct order\n1624 |         if compute_uv:\n1625 |             s, u = eigh(a)\n1626 |             sgn = sign(s)\n1627 |             s = abs(s)\n1628 |             sidx = argsort(s)[..., ::-1]\n1629 |             sgn = _nx.take_along_axis(sgn, sidx, axis=-1)\n1630 |             s = _nx.take_along_axis(s, sidx, axis=-1)\n1631 |             u = _nx.take_along_axis(u, sidx[..., None, :], axis=-1)\n1632 |             # singular values are unsigned, move the sign into v\n1633 |             vt = transpose(u * sgn[..., None, :]).conjugate()\n1634 |             return wrap(u), s, wrap(vt)\n1635 |         else:\n1636 |             s = eigvalsh(a)\n1637 |             s = s[..., ::-1]\n1638 |             s = abs(s)\n1639 |             return sort(s)[..., ::-1]\n1640 | \n1641 |     _assert_stacked_2d(a)\n1642 |     t, result_t = _commonType(a)\n1643 | \n1644 |     extobj = get_linalg_error_extobj(_raise_linalgerror_svd_nonconvergence)\n1645 | \n1646 |     m, n = a.shape[-2:]\n1647 |     if compute_uv:\n1648 |         if full_matrices:\n1649 |             if m < n:\n1650 |                 gufunc = _umath_linalg.svd_m_f\n1651 |             else:\n1652 |                 gufunc = _umath_linalg.svd_n_f\n1653 |         else:\n1654 |             if m < n:\n1655 |                 gufunc = _umath_linalg.svd_m_s\n1656 |             else:\n1657 |                 gufunc = _umath_linalg.svd_n_s\n1658 | \n1659 |         signature = 'D->DdD' if isComplexType(t) else 'd->ddd'\n1660 |         u, s, vh = gufunc(a, signature=signature, extobj=extobj)\n1661 |         u = u.astype(result_t, copy=False)\n1662 |         s = s.astype(_realType(result_t), copy=False)\n1663 |         vh = vh.astype(result_t, copy=False)\n1664 |         return wrap(u), s, wrap(vh)\n1665 |     else:\n1666 |         if m < n:\n1667 |             gufunc = _umath_linalg.svd_m\n1668 |         else:\n1669 |             gufunc = _umath_linalg.svd_n\n1670 | \n1671 |         signature = 'D->d' if isComplexType(t) else 'd->d'\n1672 |         s = gufunc(a, signature=signature, extobj=extobj)\n1673 |         s = s.astype(_realType(result_t), copy=False)\n1674 |         return s\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"sliding_window_view\", \"sliding_window sliding_window_view\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `numpy/distutils/line_endings.py:unix2dos`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n43 | def unix2dos(file):\n44 |     \"Replace LF with CRLF in argument files.  Print names of changed files.\"\n45 |     if os.path.isdir(file):\n46 |         print(file, \"Directory!\")\n47 |         return\n48 | \n49 |     with open(file, \"rb\") as fp:\n50 |         data = fp.read()\n51 |     if '\\0' in data:\n52 |         print(file, \"Binary!\")\n53 |         return\n54 |     newdata = re.sub(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\", data)\n55 |     newdata = re.sub(\"\\n\", \"\\r\\n\", newdata)\n56 |     if newdata != data:\n57 |         print('unix2dos:', file)\n58 |         with open(file, \"wb\") as f:\n59 |             f.write(newdata)\n60 |         return file\n61 |     else:\n62 |         print(file, 'ok')\n```\n\nFound file `numpy/distutils/line_endings.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Search `numpy/distutils/line_endings.py` for the full content if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"as_strided\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py:as_strided`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `as_strided`.\n```\n 37 | def as_strided(x, shape=None, strides=None, subok=False, writeable=True):\n 38 |     \"\"\"\n 39 |     Create a view into the array with the given shape and strides.\n 40 | \n 41 |     .. warning:: This function has to be used with extreme care, see notes.\n 42 | \n 43 |     Parameters\n 44 |     ----------\n 45 |     x : ndarray\n 46 |         Array to create a new.\n 47 |     shape : sequence of int, optional\n 48 |         The shape of the new array. Defaults to ``x.shape``.\n 49 |     strides : sequence of int, optional\n 50 |         The strides of the new array. Defaults to ``x.strides``.\n 51 |     subok : bool, optional\n 52 |         .. versionadded:: 1.10\n 53 | \n 54 |         If True, subclasses are preserved.\n 55 |     writeable : bool, optional\n 56 |         .. versionadded:: 1.12\n 57 | \n 58 |         If set to False, the returned array will always be readonly.\n 59 |         Otherwise it will be writable if the original array was. It\n 60 |         is advisable to set this to False if possible (see Notes).\n 61 | \n 62 |     Returns\n 63 |     -------\n 64 |     view : ndarray\n 65 | \n 66 |     See also\n 67 |     --------\n 68 |     broadcast_to: broadcast an array to a given shape.\n 69 |     reshape : reshape an array.\n 70 | \n 71 |     Notes\n 72 |     -----\n 73 |     ``as_strided`` creates a view into the array given the exact strides\n 74 |     and shape. This means it manipulates the internal data structure of\n 75 |     ndarray and, if done incorrectly, the array elements can point to\n 76 |     invalid memory and can corrupt results or crash your program.\n 77 |     It is advisable to always use the original ``x.strides`` when\n 78 |     calculating new strides to avoid reliance on a contiguous memory\n 79 |     layout.\n 80 | \n 81 |     Furthermore, arrays created with this function often contain self\n 82 |     overlapping memory, so that two elements are identical.\n 83 |     Vectorized write operations on such arrays will typically be\n 84 |     unpredictable. They may even give different results for small, large,\n 85 |     or transposed arrays.\n 86 |     Since writing to these arrays has to be tested and done with great\n 87 |     care, you may want to use ``writeable=False`` to avoid accidental write\n 88 |     operations.\n 89 | \n 90 |     For these reasons it is advisable to avoid ``as_strided`` when\n 91 |     possible.\n 92 |     \"\"\"\n 93 |     # first convert input to array, possibly keeping subclass\n 94 |     x = np.array(x, copy=False, subok=subok)\n 95 |     interface = dict(x.__array_interface__)\n 96 |