             Keyword arguments to be passed to matplotlib's ``plot``.\n1040 | \n1041 |         Returns\n1042 |         -------\n1043 |         display : :class:`~sklearn.calibration.CalibrationDisplay`\n1044 |             Object that stores computed values and creates plot\n1045 |         \"\"\"\n1046 |         check_matplotlib_support(\"CalibrationDisplay.plot\")\n1047 | \n1048 |         import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n1049 | \n1050 |         if ax is None:\n1051 |             _, ax = plt.subplots()\n1052 | \n1053 |         name = self.estimator_name if name is None else name\n1054 | \n1055 |         line_kwargs = {\n1056 |             \"label\": name,\n1057 |             \"marker\": \".\",\n1058 |         }\n1059 |         line_kwargs.update(**kwargs)\n1060 | \n1061 |         # Plot perfectly calibrated\n1062 |         ax.plot([0, 1], [0, 1], linestyle=\"--\", color=\"gray\", label=\"Perfectly calibrated\")\n1063 | \n1064 |         # Plot calibration curve\n1065 |         ax.plot(self.prob_pred, self.prob_true, **line_kwargs)\n1066 | \n1067 |         ax.set(xlabel=\"Mean predicted probability\", ylabel=\"Fraction of positives\")\n1068 |         ax.legend(loc=\"best\")\n1069 | \n1070 |         return self\n```\n\n##Searching for term \"CalibrationDisplay.from_estimator\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:CalibrationDisplay.from_estimator`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1081 |     @classmethod\n1082 |     def from_estimator(cls, estimator, X, y, *, pos_label=None, n_bins=5,\n1083 |                        strategy=\"uniform\", name=None, sample_weight=None, ax=None):\n1084 |         \"\"\"Plot calibration curve for a given classifier.\n1085 | \n1086 |         Extra keyword arguments will be passed to matplotlib's ``plot``.\n1087 | \n1088 |         Parameters\n1089 |         ----------\n1090 |         estimator : estimator instance\n1091 |             Fitted classifier or a fitted :class:`~sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline`\n1092 |             in which the last estimator is a classifier.\n1093 | \n1094 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n1095 |             Training data.\n1096 | \n1097 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n1098 |             True targets.\n1099 | \n1100 |         pos_label : str or int, default=None\n1101 |             The class considered as the positive class when computing\n1102 |             the metrics. By default, `estimators.classes_[1]` is\n1103 |             considered as the positive class.\n1104 | \n1105 |         n_bins : int, default=5\n1106 |             Number of bins to discretize the [0, 1] interval. A bigger number\n1107 |             requires more data. Bins with no samples (i.e. without\n1108 |             corresponding values in `y_prob`) will not be returned, thus the\n1109 |             returned arrays may have less than `n_bins` values.\n1110 | \n1111 |         strategy : {'uniform', 'quantile'}, default='uniform'\n1112 |             Strategy used to define the widths of the bins.\n1113 | \n1114 |             'uniform'\n1115 |                 The bins have identical widths.\n1116 |             'quantile'\n1117 |                 The bins have the same number of samples and depend on `y_prob`.\n1118 | \n1119 |         name : str, default=None\n1120 |             Name of estimator to be displayed in the legend. If `None`,\n1121 |             the name of the estimator is used.\n1122 | \n1123 |         sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n1124 |             Sample weights.\n1125 | \n1126 |         ax : matplotlib Axes, default=None\n1127 |             Axes object to plot on. If `None`, a new figure and axes is created.\n1128 | \n1129 |         **kwargs : dict\n1130 |             Keyword arguments to be passed to matplotlib's ``plot``.\n1131 | \n1132 |         Returns\n1133 |         -------\n1134 |         display : :class:`~sklearn.calibration.CalibrationDisplay`\n1135 |             Object that stores computed values and creates plot\n1136 |         \"\"\"\n1137 |         method_name = f\"{cls.__name__}.from_estimator\"\n1138 |         check_matplotlib_support(method_name)\n1139 | \n1140 |         import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n1141 | \n1142 |         if not is_classifier(estimator):\n1143 |             raise ValueError(\n1144 |                 f\"'{estimator.__class__.__name__}' is not a classifier. \"\n1145 |                 f\"{method_name} only accepts classifiers.\"\n1146 |             )\n1147 | \n1148 |         prob_true, prob_pred = calibration_curve(\n1149 |             y, estimator.predict_proba(X)[:, 1], n_bins=n_bins,\n1150 |             strategy=strategy, sample_weight=sample_weight\n1151 |         )\n1152 | \n1153 |         if ax is None:\n1154 |             _, ax = plt.subplots()\n1155 | \n1156 |         name = estimator.__class__.__name__ if name is None else name\n1157 | \n1158 |         return cls(prob_true, prob_pred, y_prob=None, estimator_name=name,\n1159 |                      pos_label=pos_label, ax=ax).plot(name=name)\n```\n\n##Searching for term \"CalibrationDisplay.from_predictions\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/calibration.py:CalibrationDisplay.from_predictions`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1200 |     @classmethod\n1201 |     def from_predictions(cls, prob_true, prob_pred, *, name=None, ax=None):\n1202 |         \"\"\"Plot calibration curve given the true and predicted probabilities.\n1203 | \n1204 |         Extra keyword arguments will be passed to matplotlib's ``plot``.\n1205 | \n1206 |         Parameters\n1207 |         ----------\n1208 |         prob_true : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n1209 |             The proportion of samples whose class is the positive class, in each\n1210 |             bin (fraction of positives).\n1211 | \n1212 |         prob_pred : ndarray of shape (n_bins,) or smaller\n1213 |             The mean predicted p